    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 6,
        "sourceEnd": 162,
        "text": "Welcome to The Campus Signal. I'm Maya, with media tutor Liam Foster. Today's examples come from a simulated class survey and an invented newsroom exercise.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "07380208f1e1d66f1c0492958a4e9b76f4b267c329c0a22274897b58571a9273",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 169,
        "sourceEnd": 393,
        "text": "In our simulated survey, most respondents shared content because they trusted the person who posted it, not because they had verified it themselves. But some checked the original posts. We shouldn't label all users careless.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "d38f667acac2618d1f2a1d7c98ed98a60749f3a773a12356850943cf6c4bdb70",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 400,
        "sourceEnd": 456,
        "text": "Do newsrooms have equal resources to verify information?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "5e0d9c9c4180acea8f63a78ec46321ffb33e94fc20d76b28bc55510c6bfae342",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 463,
        "sourceEnd": 706,
        "text": "No. In our example, the national paper has six fact-checkers, while the local blog has one. Both follow the rule: check before you publish. Their capacity differs, so a sweeping statement about every newsroom would hide important distinctions.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "b576f2a968521cf263f062be4b1c3bc7adf0a565670ba6847aeb339177ac0f50",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 713,
        "sourceEnd": 751,
        "text": "Why avoid writing 'all experts agree'?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "e6547cbbbff1ebcdc79da1673403bdb01a80fc56acbbec4535626d092e8855e8",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 758,
        "sourceEnd": 965,
        "text": "Our simulated panel included disagreement. We could report what several speakers argued, but a universal claim would require evidence about every relevant expert. Agreement among a few speakers isn't enough.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "9740095e8175468c90e2ceb6b94b4f01fe7be82f5050416094baf7d3c69fc992",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 972,
        "sourceEnd": 1051,
        "text": "What about reporting verbs? Suppose a mayor says, 'I believe crime is falling.'",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "7b0bfe95af5913909321cfe02110006cbdb113aea5cc623671aa0dd999db9028",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 1058,
        "sourceEnd": 1217,
        "text": "Calling that a claim can signal distance from the assertion. 'Claimed' doesn't by itself prove the assertion false. 'Said' or 'stated' is usually more neutral.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "52de4ad3a04321c0dd26aaa62eb3ae51fa9d2df3151cae58de2675eb2c3bdecb",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 1224,
        "sourceEnd": 1239,
        "text": "And 'admitted'?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "c80738d375d73593c510f03c8fbb0638c0a2ff64eabaa1005dc5413d47f60e78",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 1246,
        "sourceEnd": 1517,
        "text": "Compare 'The company admitted that there had been an error' with 'The company stated that there had been an error'. The first presents an acknowledgement of something unwelcome; the second reports it neutrally. 'Admit' doesn't always prove that the speaker was reluctant.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "a2b61ee5a0d071a8e08a23ce5f21810405cdbf3e4fcc0d1975e764099d160be7",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 1524,
        "sourceEnd": 1584,
        "text": "What mistake should students watch for when they paraphrase?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "4c16584421fbd7033055d33c75d6081cd3d66d4eaadeed7b869c16b284982350",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 1591,
        "sourceEnd": 1816,
        "text": "Changing the original meaning by accident. If a source says 'some participants reported fatigue', changing 'some' to 'all' would distort the evidence. A paraphrase changes the wording while preserving the claim and its scope.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "b33f172b16cb7f73a685364a4abbfe416fa8f200078ce5c429b259d1b763a28a",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 1823,
        "sourceEnd": 1857,
        "text": "Can you explain those scope words?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "ebe578522b4e9d23503f4fca361f6fd1489da7c6a07294859ba70c25ed99c310",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 1864,
        "sourceEnd": 2028,
        "text": "'Some' means an unspecified portion. 'Most' means more than half, but that word alone doesn't establish that every person is included. 'All' means every single one.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "553446ad2b23a682da72b7eb66e96b29746673352f18fb95f57e0b56fa7ab73d",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "host",
        "speaker": "Host",
        "sourceStart": 2035,
        "sourceEnd": 2074,
        "text": "Any final advice to avoid being misled?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-AriaNeural",
        "textHash": "5de5c59d0004c4968e3b884de90a78d83e5f6d15edcc6b9acd9eba8926173ad7",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      },
      {
        "role": "liam",
        "speaker": "Liam",
        "sourceStart": 2081,
        "sourceEnd": 2256,
        "text": "Ask yourself: What is the evidence? Who said it? And what exact words did they use? Check those before sharing, especially when a headline sounds more certain than its source.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-GuyNeural",
        "textHash": "fe3a40081252a6916022c4459e7594b1c18fad9b7ce0fdf780eba093d1430230",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.65
      }
    ]
  },
